Third-Party Dependencies:

This repository references the following third-party components as git
submodules and/or as tools resolved/built at build time. Each component
retains its own upstream license; consult the respective project for the
full terms.

- OpenC910 (rtl/openc910)
  XuanTie / T-Head OpenC910 RISC-V core (worked-example DUT).
  Submodule currently points at a Tenstorrent contributor's personal fork,
  https://github.com/akumartt/openc910 (branch tt-mmu-fixes), itself forked
  from https://github.com/XUANTIE-RV/openc910.
  License: Apache-2.0 (confirmed in both the upstream and fork LICENSE files).
  NOTE: before public release this submodule should be repointed at an
  official Tenstorrent-controlled fork/mirror rather than a personal account,
  so the dependency does not disappear if that personal account is removed
  or renamed.

- mem-manager (external/mem-manager)
  Tenstorrent USA, Inc.
  Upstream: https://github.com/tenstorrent/mem-manager
  License: Apache-2.0 (confirmed).

- riescue (external/riescue)
  Tenstorrent USA, Inc. page-table / test generator.
  Upstream: https://github.com/tenstorrent/riescue
  License: Apache-2.0 (confirmed).

- Verilator (resolved via VERILATOR_HOME/PATH, or auto-built from source into
  the gitignored external/verilator/ if neither is found; not vendored or
  distributed with this repository)
  Verilator SystemVerilog simulator.
  Upstream: https://github.com/verilator/verilator
  License: LGPL-3.0-only OR Artistic-2.0 (dual-licensed at the user's option;
  confirmed from upstream). Used only as an external build/simulation tool;
  no Verilator source is copied into or distributed with this repository.

- Whisper reference model (external/tt-whisper-mmu)
  RISC-V instruction-set simulator (nests the swerv-iss "whisper" submodule)
  used as the golden reference model for translation scoring.
  Upstream: https://github.com/tenstorrent/tt-whisper-mmu
  License: Apache-2.0 (confirmed).
  NOTE: this repository is still private/internal as of this writing. Its
  submodule URL uses SSH (see .gitmodules) and MUST be repointed to the
  public HTTPS URL — and the upstream repository itself made public — before
  this repository is released publicly, or anonymous clones will fail.
